/* I thought suddenly, what if i keep returning boolean on the recursive stack?
   since they mention in the question root to leaf, "the leaf" carry the sums to the
   base case (terminating condition) and checks and return boolean result.
   
    If i go till 7 (a leaf) then check with 2 (a path for another leaf), then going back, would i have to modify sum?  Wait a sec...

    You know that at level in the path we have different versions/values of sum, which we can't change directly, and in this case we don't have to. So no need to subtract 7, when we go back to 11 and to 2.

    To put it technical term, each recursive stack has it's own sum. We don't have to subtract anything to traverback for explorting other paths.

*/

class Solution {
    public boolean verifyPathSum(TreeNode node, int targetSum, int sum){
        if(node == null){
            if(sum == targetSum){
                return true;
                break;					//error : break statement outside switch or loop 
            }
            return false;
        }

        sum+=node.val;
        verifyPathSum(node.left, targetSum, sum);
        verifyPathSum(node.right, targetSum, sum);

        return false;

    }

    public boolean hasPathSum(TreeNode root, int targetSum) {
        return verifyPathSum(root, targetSum, 0);
    }
}

